### MTO Stock Inventory API - IntelliJ HTTP Client
@host = localhost
@port = 8080
@apiVersion = v1
@baseUrl = http://{{host}}:{{port}}/api/{{apiVersion}}/inventory
@materialId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a020
@secondMaterialId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a021
@warehouseId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a001
@targetWarehouseId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a002
@projectId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a010
@supplierId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a030
@assemblyId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a040
@movementId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a050
@reservationId = 018f60be-1b9a-7cc3-8c6b-2f93e8c6a060
@keycloakUrl = http://auth.mto.local:8082
@realm = mto
@clientId = mto-frontend
@username = almacen.operario
@password = local

### Auth - Get access token from Keycloak
# La API no emite tokens: los emite Keycloak y aqui solo se validan. Ejecuta esta peticion primero;
# el token queda en {{accessToken}} y lo usan todas las demas.
# Los usuarios de arriba son los del realm local que importa docker-compose (keycloak/README.md):
# almacen.lector (solo lectura), almacen.operario (lectura y escritura) y almacen.responsable
# (ademas cancela reservas y ajusta inventario). Todos con contrasena 'local'.
POST {{keycloakUrl}}/realms/{{realm}}/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id={{clientId}}&username={{username}}&password={{password}}

> {%
    client.global.set("accessToken", response.body.access_token);
%}

### Materials - Create material
POST {{baseUrl}}/materials
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "MAT-COPPER-50",
  "name": "Copper catenary wire 50 mm2",
  "unitOfMeasure": "m",
  "minimumStockLevel": 250.000000
}

### Materials - Update material
PUT {{baseUrl}}/materials/{{materialId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "code": "MAT-COPPER-50",
  "name": "Copper catenary wire 50 mm2 - updated",
  "unitOfMeasure": "m",
  "minimumStockLevel": 300.000000,
  "active": true
}

### Materials - Get material
GET {{baseUrl}}/materials/{{materialId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json

### Materials - Search materials
GET {{baseUrl}}/materials?name=Copper&active=true&page=0&size=20&sort=code,asc
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json

### Materials - Low stock
GET {{baseUrl}}/materials/low-stock?warehouseId={{warehouseId}}&page=0&size=20&sort=code,asc
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json

### Materials - Get material stock
GET {{baseUrl}}/materials/{{materialId}}/stock?warehouseId={{warehouseId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json

### Materials - Get material movements
GET {{baseUrl}}/materials/{{materialId}}/movements?warehouseId={{warehouseId}}&dateFrom=2026-08-01T00:00:00Z&dateTo=2026-08-31T23:59:59Z&page=0&size=20&sort=occurredAt,desc
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json

### Materials - Get material change history
# Ojo con la de arriba: /movements es el libro mayor de existencias -que entro y que salio- y
# /revisions es el historial del registro -quien cambio el stock minimo, la unidad o la baja logica,
# y que decia antes-. Son dos preguntas distintas. Ver docs/07-auditing.md.
GET {{baseUrl}}/materials/{{materialId}}/revisions?page=0&size=20
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json

